Phase 0: Global context (include in every session)
`

text
You are a senior full-stack engineer building a movie, film, animation and series streaming website (similar in spirit to zarfilm.com or plan.ir). The app lets visitors browse and watch uploaded media, with optional user accounts. There is NO payment, subscription, or billing in this project.

## Tech stack (fixed, do not change)
- Backend: NestJS with TypeScript (strict mode)
- Frontend: Astro (TypeScript), using SSR mode with the @astrojs/node adapter, plus Astro islands (React or Svelte, pick one and stay consistent) for interactive parts such as the video player, search, and admin forms
- Database: SQLite, accessed through Prisma ORM (file: ./data/app.db)
- Auth: JWT access tokens (short-lived, 15 min) + rotating refresh tokens (7 days), stored in httpOnly, Secure (in production), SameSite=Lax cookies. Passwords hashed with argon2 (or bcrypt cost 12 if argon2 is unavailable).
- Validation: class-validator + class-transformer on the backend, Zod or equivalent on the frontend forms
- API docs: Swagger (@nestjs/swagger) at /api/docs
- Package manager: pnpm. Monorepo layout:
/apps/api (NestJS)

/apps/web (Astro)

/packages/shared (shared TypeScript types and constants, e.g. roles, media types, enums)

/storage (uploaded media files, git-ignored)

/data (SQLite database file, git-ignored)

text
- Language of the UI: English by default, but all text must go through an i18n-ready layer (a simple JSON dictionary) so Persian (RTL) can be added later. Support `dir="rtl"` switching in the layout but do not translate content yet.

## Engineering rules
- Never store secrets in code. Use a `.env.example` in each app and document every variable.
- Every endpoint must have a DTO, validation, and a Swagger decorator.
- Use a global exception filter returning a consistent error shape: `{ statusCode, error, message, path, timestamp }`.
- Write unit tests for services and e2e tests for critical API flows (Jest / Supertest for API, Vitest for web utilities).
- Keep code modular: one NestJS module per domain (auth, users, catalog, media, uploads, streaming, admin).
- Do not leave TODO placeholders in the final output of a phase. If something is out of scope, say so explicitly in the phase summary.
- At the end of each phase, provide: (1) a list of files created/changed, (2) how to run it, (3) how to test it, (4) what was verified, (5) known limitations.

## Domain vocabulary (use these exact names everywhere)
- **Title**: any watchable item. Has a `type`: MOVIE, FILM, ANIMATION, or SERIES.
  - MOVIE and FILM are single-video titles. (Use FILM for art/independent films and MOVIE for mainstream; both are stored the same way and only differ by category.)
  - ANIMATION is a single or multi-episode animated work (may be a movie or a series; use `isSeries` flag for this).
  - SERIES is a multi-season show, made of Seasons, which contain Episodes.
- **Category**: a browsable grouping. Seeded defaults: Movies, Films, Animations, Series. Admins can create more. A Title can belong to many Categories (many-to-many).
- **Genre**: tags such as Action, Drama, Comedy, Horror, Sci-Fi, Documentary, Romance, Thriller, Family. Many-to-many with Title.
- **Season** and **Episode**: belong to a SERIES title (or an ANIMATION with isSeries=true). Episodes have their own video file.
- **MediaFile**: a stored video file with metadata (original filename, storage path, mime type, size in bytes, duration in seconds, resolution, status).
- **Asset**: poster image, backdrop image, or subtitle (.vtt) file linked to a Title or Episode.
- **User**: has `role`: USER or ADMIN (EDITOR is optional, see Phase 1).
- Visitors who are not logged in are "guests" and can browse and watch everything that is published.

## Required content states
Every Title has a `status`: DRAFT (only admins see it), PUBLISHED (visible to everyone), ARCHIVED (hidden from lists, but direct link still works for admins only).
`
Phase 1: Foundation and Authentication and Authorization
`

text
Build Phase 1 of the project described in the global context. Scope: monorepo setup, database schema for users, auth, roles, and the basic web shell. Do NOT build the catalog or uploads yet.

## 1. Monorepo and tooling
- Set up pnpm workspaces with apps/api, apps/web, packages/shared.
- Configure ESLint, Prettier, and a root `pnpm dev` script that runs both apps.
- API runs on port 4000, web on 4321. CORS allows only the web origin, with credentials enabled.
- Add helmet, a global ValidationPipe (whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true, transform: true), and cookie-parser.

## 2. Database (Prisma + SQLite)
Create the `User` and `RefreshToken` models, plus the initial migration:
- User: id (cuid), email (unique, lowercased), username (unique, 3–30 chars, alphanumeric and underscore), passwordHash, displayName, avatarUrl (nullable), role (enum: USER, ADMIN), isEmailVerified (boolean, default false), isActive (boolean, default true), createdAt, updatedAt, lastLoginAt (nullable).
- RefreshToken: id, userId (FK, cascade delete), tokenHash (SHA-256 of the token, never store raw), familyId (for reuse detection), expiresAt, revokedAt (nullable), replacedById (nullable), createdAt, userAgent, ip.
- Seed script: creates one ADMIN from env vars ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_PASSWORD. Idempotent. If the variables are missing, the seed prints a warning and skips.

## 3. Authentication endpoints (`/api/auth`)
- POST `/register`: email, username, password (min 8 chars, must contain letter and number), displayName. Creates a USER. Returns the public user profile and sets cookies. Rate limited.
- POST `/login`: email or username + password. Generic error message on failure ("Invalid credentials") to avoid account enumeration. Lock an account for 15 minutes after 5 failed attempts within 15 minutes (track in a `failedLoginAttempts` and `lockedUntil` field on User; add these fields to the schema). Rate limited.
- POST `/refresh`: reads refresh cookie, rotates it (issues a new refresh token, revokes the old one, links replacedById). If a revoked token is reused, revoke the ENTIRE token family and return 401 (reuse detection).
- POST `/logout`: revokes the current refresh token and clears cookies.
- POST `/logout-all`: requires auth; revokes all of the user's refresh tokens.
- GET `/me`: returns the current user.
- POST `/change-password`: requires auth and current password; revokes all other sessions.
- Forgot/reset password: POST `/forgot-password` always returns 200 (no enumeration). It creates a one-time reset token (hashed, 30-minute expiry) and logs the reset link to the console in development (use an `EmailService` interface with a console implementation; real SMTP is out of scope).
- Email verification: implement the same token pattern (`/verify-email?token=`). Unverified users can log in and browse, but cannot be given any write permissions in later phases (for now, just expose the flag).

## 4. Token and cookie details
- Access token JWT payload: `sub`, `role`, `iat`, `exp`. Signed with HS256 using JWT_ACCESS_SECRET. Min 32 chars.
- Refresh token: 64 random bytes, base64url. Sent only as httpOnly cookie named `rt`, path `/api/auth`. Access token cookie named `at`, path `/`.
- Cookie flags: httpOnly, sameSite=lax, secure when NODE_ENV=production.
- JwtAuthGuard reads the `at` cookie (also accept `Authorization: Bearer` for API clients and Swagger).

## 5. Authorization (RBAC)
- Decorators: `@Public()` (skips auth), `@Roles(Role.ADMIN)`, `@CurrentUser()`.
- Global guards applied in this order: JwtAuthGuard (default-deny: every route requires auth unless @Public), then RolesGuard.
- Optional auth: `@OptionalAuth()` decorator for routes that show extra data to logged-in users but work for guests. Guests get `null` for user-specific fields.
- Define a permission matrix in `packages/shared` and document it in `docs/permissions.md`:
  - Guest: browse and watch PUBLISHED content.
  - USER: all guest rights + profile edit, watch history (added later), favorites (added later).
  - ADMIN: everything, including DRAFT/ARCHIVED content, uploads, category/genre management, user management.
- Ownership check helper for resources owned by a user (used later for favorites and history).
- Account deactivation: if `isActive` is false, the JWT guard rejects the request even with a valid token.

## 6. User management (admin only)
- GET `/api/admin/users` (paginated, search by email/username, filter by role).
- PATCH `/api/admin/users/:id/role` (cannot demote yourself; last admin cannot be demoted).
- PATCH `/api/admin/users/:id/active` (deactivate/reactivate; deactivation revokes all refresh tokens).

## 7. User profile
- PATCH `/api/users/me`: displayName, avatarUrl (URL validation for now; upload comes later).
- DELETE `/api/users/me`: soft-delete by setting isActive=false and anonymizing email and username; revoke tokens.

## 8. Web shell (apps/web)
- Astro SSR with middleware that reads the `at` cookie, validates by calling the API (or verifies the JWT locally with the same secret), and sets `Astro.locals.user`. If the access token is expired but the refresh cookie exists, call `/api/auth/refresh` server-side and forward the new cookies to the browser.
- Pages: `/` (placeholder home), `/login`, `/register`, `/forgot-password`, `/reset-password`, `/verify-email`, `/account` (profile edit, change password, logout all sessions).
- Protected route helper: pages under /account and /admin redirect to /login?next=... if not logged in. /admin requires ADMIN and returns 403 page otherwise.
- Header shows Login/Register for guests and avatar menu for users. Menu items for admins: "Admin".
- Basic responsive layout with a dark theme and CSS variables. Keep styling minimal but clean.

## 9. Tests (required)
- Unit: password policy, token hashing, refresh rotation, reuse detection, lockout logic, RolesGuard.
- E2E: register → login → /me → refresh → logout; reuse of old refresh token revokes family; guest cannot access /api/admin/users (401); user gets 403 on admin routes; admin can access; deactivated user is rejected.

## Acceptance criteria for Phase 1
- `pnpm install && pnpm db:migrate && pnpm db:seed && pnpm dev` works from a clean clone.
- All tests pass with `pnpm test`.
- Swagger at http://localhost:4000/api/docs lists all auth endpoints with examples.
- The phase summary follows the format in Phase 0.
`
Phase 2: Catalog, Categories, Uploads, and Streaming
`

text
Build Phase 2. Phase 1 (auth, roles, monorepo, Prisma setup, web shell) is complete. Do not change its public behavior except where noted.

## 1. Database additions (Prisma migration)
- Category: id, name, slug (unique), description, coverImage (nullable), sortOrder (int), isActive (bool), createdAt, updatedAt. Seed: Movies (slug movies), Films (films), Animations (animations), Series (series).
- Genre: id, name (unique), slug (unique).
- Title: id, slug (unique, auto-generated from title, with numeric suffix on collision), type (enum MOVIE, FILM, ANIMATION, SERIES), originalTitle (nullable), title, synopsis, releaseYear, runtimeMinutes (nullable for series), ageRating (nullable, e.g. G, PG, PG-13, R, or free text), language, country (nullable), status (DRAFT, PUBLISHED, ARCHIVED), isSeries (bool, true for SERIES and series-type ANIMATION), featured (bool), viewCount (int, default 0), publishedAt (nullable), createdById, createdAt, updatedAt. Add indexes on status, type, releaseYear, publishedAt.
- TitleCategory (join table), TitleGenre (join table). Both have composite primary keys.
- Season: id, titleId (FK cascade), number (unique per title), name (nullable), synopsis (nullable), releaseYear (nullable).
- Episode: id, seasonId (FK cascade), number (unique per season), title, synopsis (nullable), runtimeMinutes (nullable), airDate (nullable), status, createdAt, updatedAt.
- MediaFile: id, titleId (nullable FK), episodeId (nullable FK), originalName, storageKey (path relative to /storage, never exposed to clients), mimeType, sizeBytes (bigint or int), durationSeconds (nullable), width, height (nullable), codec (nullable), checksumSha256, status (UPLOADING, PROCESSING, READY, FAILED), errorMessage (nullable), createdAt. Exactly one of titleId/episodeId must be set (enforce in service and with a CHECK constraint in the migration).
- Asset: id, titleId or episodeId, kind (POSTER, BACKDROP, SUBTITLE), language (nullable, for subtitles), storageKey, mimeType, sizeBytes, createdAt.
- WatchProgress and Favorite are NOT part of this phase.

## 2. Categories and genres
- Public: GET `/api/categories` (active only, sorted by sortOrder), GET `/api/categories/:slug` (with paginated titles), GET `/api/genres`.
- Admin: POST, PATCH, DELETE for categories (DELETE is blocked if the category still has titles; use `isActive=false` instead) and for genres. Admins can reorder with PATCH `/api/admin/categories/reorder` taking an ordered array of ids.
- Category uses a sortOrder field and the frontend shows them in that order.

## 3. Titles API
- Public (guests + users): GET `/api/titles` with query params: page, pageSize (max 50), type, categorySlug, genreSlug, year (or yearFrom/yearTo), language, country, sort (newest, popular, title_asc, year_desc), q (search on title, originalTitle, synopsis; use SQLite LIKE with an escape helper, or FTS5 if you can configure it through a Prisma raw migration; document the choice). Only PUBLISHED titles are returned to non-admins.
- GET `/api/titles/:slug`: full detail with categories, genres, assets (poster/backdrop URLs), and seasons/episodes for series. Non-admins get 404 for non-published titles.
- GET `/api/titles/featured` and GET `/api/titles/latest` and GET `/api/titles/popular` (popular uses viewCount, last 30 days if you implement a view event table; otherwise total viewCount, and document it).
- Admin (under `/api/admin/titles`): create, update, delete (soft delete to ARCHIVED then hard delete allowed only for DRAFT), change status, list all statuses with filters, set featured.
- Admin series management: CRUD for seasons and episodes under `/api/admin/titles/:id/seasons` and `/api/admin/seasons/:id/episodes`.
- Public series endpoints: GET `/api/titles/:slug/seasons/:seasonNumber/episodes`.

## 4. Upload pipeline (admin only)
Uploads are the riskiest part. Implement carefully.
- Use multipart uploads with `@fastify`-free Nest setup: use Express with multer (diskStorage into `/storage/tmp/`). Do not buffer whole files in memory.
- Limits from env: MAX_VIDEO_SIZE_MB (default 8192), MAX_IMAGE_SIZE_MB (default 10), MAX_SUBTITLE_SIZE_MB (default 2).
- Allowed types, validated by magic bytes (file-type package), NOT by extension or client mime:
  - Video: mp4 (video/mp4), mkv (video/x-matroska), webm (video/webm)
  - Image: jpeg, png, webp
  - Subtitle: .vtt (WEBVTT header check) and .srt (converted to .vtt on save)
- Upload flow:
  1. POST `/api/admin/media/upload` (multipart: file, plus fields titleId or episodeId, kind). Creates MediaFile with status UPLOADING, writes to temp, validates, moves to `/storage/videos/{yyyy}/{mm}/{uuid}.{ext}`, computes SHA-256 while streaming, sets status PROCESSING.
  2. A background worker (BullMQ is out of scope. Use an in-process queue with a simple DB-polling job runner, or node-cron every 10s, your choice; document it) runs ffprobe (from the ffmpeg install) to read duration, width, height, codec. If ffprobe is missing, mark status READY with null metadata and log a warning. Set READY or FAILED.
  3. Reject duplicate checksums for the same title/episode with 409.
- Videos are never served from the `/storage` path directly. They are served only through the streaming endpoint.
- Filenames from users are sanitized and never used as storage paths.
- Delete media: removes DB row and file.
- Admin list endpoint for media with status filters, and GET `/api/admin/media/:id/status` for polling.

## 5. Streaming and images
- GET `/api/stream/:mediaId` (guests and users, but only if the parent title is PUBLISHED, and the media is READY):
  - Supports HTTP Range requests (206 Partial Content, Accept-Ranges: bytes, Content-Range). This is required for seeking.
  - Sets Content-Type from the stored mime type, Cache-Control: private, max-age=3600.
  - Rate limit per IP to slow down scraping (e.g. 300 requests/min per IP for stream).
  - Optional: signed short-lived stream URL (HMAC with `?exp=&sig=`) generated by GET `/api/titles/:slug/playback` for an episode or movie. Implement this, it prevents hotlinking. The stream endpoint validates the signature when STREAM_SIGNING_SECRET is set.
  - Stream count increments viewCount once per playback session (not per range request). Use a short-lived in-memory dedupe or a playbackId in the signed URL.
- GET `/api/images/:assetId`: serves poster/backdrop/subtitle with caching headers. Subtitle endpoint sets Content-Type text/vtt.
- Future-proofing: streaming code must live behind a `StorageService` interface so S3 can be added later. Note this in docs.

## 6. Search and listing performance
- Add pagination everywhere (cursor or offset, document which).
- Add DB indexes for the filter columns.
- Add an in-memory LRU cache (or a simple TTL cache) for `/api/categories` and `/api/genres` with invalidation on write.

## 7. Seed data (for development only, behind SEED_DEMO=true)
- 3 categories populated with demo titles, 1 series with 2 seasons and 3 episodes each.
- Demo media: since real video is not included, generate a 5-second test MP4 with ffmpeg in the seed script if ffmpeg exists; otherwise skip media and log it.

## 8. Tests
- Unit: slug generator, range header parser, file type validator (magic bytes), upload path sanitizer, signature generator/verifier, title query builder.
- E2E: admin uploads a video and it reaches READY; guest can list only PUBLISHED titles; guest gets 404 for DRAFT; stream returns 206 for a Range request; reject spoofed file (text file renamed to .mp4) with 415; reject duplicate checksum with 409; signed URL with tampered sig returns 403.

## Acceptance criteria for Phase 2
- Admin can create a movie, attach poster and video, publish it, and it appears in `/api/titles` and in its category.
- A series with seasons and episodes is fully manageable through the API.
- Range requests work (verify with curl -r 0-1023 and check 206).
- Swagger documents every new endpoint.
- Phase summary provided in the Phase 0 format.
`
Phase 3: Web Frontend (Astro)
`

text
Build Phase 3: the public website and the admin panel UI on top of the Phase 1 and Phase 2 APIs. Use Astro SSR. Keep API calls server-side where possible (from Astro frontmatter) so the access token cookie stays httpOnly; use client islands only for interactive parts.

## 1. Public pages
- `/` Home: hero (featured title with backdrop, title, synopsis, Play and More info buttons), horizontal carousels: "Latest", "Popular", then one carousel per active Category in sortOrder (show the first 12 titles each, with a "See all" link).
- `/browse` or `/titles`: grid with filter sidebar (type, category, genre, year range, language, country, age rating), sort dropdown, search box, and pagination. Filters are URL query params so the page is shareable and SSR-rendered. Use a small island for the filter controls, while results render on the server.
- `/category/[slug]`: category page with same filter component, pre-filtered.
- `/genre/[slug]`: genre page.
- `/search?q=`: search results page with the same grid.
- `/movies`, `/films`, `/animations`, `/series`: type/category landing pages, each showing filters and a grid. (Map these to the categories with the same slugs; if categories are renamed, route by slug.)
- `/title/[slug]`: detail page. Poster, backdrop, metadata (year, runtime, language, age rating, genres), synopsis, Play button (only if the title has a playable MediaFile), and for series: season selector and episode list with thumbnails and Play per episode. Show "Coming soon" if no media is READY.
- `/watch/[slug]` and `/watch/[slug]/s/[season]/e/[episode]`: watch page with a full-width video player island (HTML5 video with native controls, keyboard shortcuts, fullscreen, subtitle track selection, playback speed, and "Next episode" button for series). Player fetches the signed playback URL from the API (`/api/titles/:slug/playback`) and sets `src`. Must support seeking (Range requests).
- Not-found and error pages: 404, 403, and 500, styled consistently.

## 2. Auth-aware UI
- Guests can browse and watch. The watch page shows a non-blocking "Sign in to save your progress" banner (saving progress is a later phase).
- Login-gated actions: none in this phase, but the layout must already support them (e.g., a "Favorites" link appears only for users; it can link to a placeholder page).
- Preserve `?next=` on login and register and redirect after success.

## 3. Admin panel (`/admin`, ADMIN only)
- Dashboard: counts of titles by status and type, media processing queue (PROCESSING/FAILED), recent uploads.
- Titles: table with search, status and type filters, create/edit form, status toggle (Draft/Published/Archived), featured toggle, delete (with confirmation).
- Title form: all fields from the Title model, multi-select for categories and genres (with inline create of a new genre), slug preview, poster and backdrop upload (drag and drop with preview).
- Series manager: seasons list, episodes list under each season, add/edit/delete, reorder episodes.
- Media uploader: drag-and-drop video upload with progress bar (use XHR or fetch with progress; the upload goes directly to the API via the proxy path, not through the browser cookie issues). After upload, poll `/api/admin/media/:id/status` until READY or FAILED. Show the filename, size, duration, and status.
- Subtitles upload per title or episode with language select.
- Categories: list with drag-and-drop reorder (save sortOrder), toggle active, create/edit.
- Users: list with search, change role, activate/deactivate. Cannot change your own role (UI disables it; API also enforces).

## 4. Forms and validation
- Use the same validation rules in the frontend (Zod) as in the backend DTOs. Put shared schemas in `packages/shared` where possible.
- Show field-level errors from the API error shape.

## 5. SEO and accessibility
- Each page has a unique `<title>`, meta description, Open Graph tags (og:image uses the poster), and a canonical URL.
- Generate `/sitemap.xml` from PUBLISHED titles and `/robots.txt` (disallow /admin, /account, /watch).
- Semantic HTML, keyboard navigable carousels, alt text from title, `prefers-reduced-motion` respected.
- Lighthouse score target: 90+ accessibility and best practices on the home page.

## 5b. Web security
- Strict Content-Security-Policy in the Astro middleware (allow self, the image domain, and media-src 'self' for the stream proxy).
- No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` for synopsis; render plain text or sanitize with a whitelist if rich text is used later.
- Forms use CSRF protection: SameSite=Lax cookies plus an Origin header check on all mutating requests in the Astro middleware.

## 6. Performance
- Astro SSR with cache headers where content is public (`s-maxage` for home and browse pages, short TTL, documented).
- Lazy-load images below the fold; use width/height attributes to avoid layout shift.
- Client JavaScript only in islands; no large global state library.

## 7. Tests
- Unit: filter-to-query-string mapping, Zod schemas, slug display helpers.
- E2E with Playwright: guest browses home → opens a title → plays a demo video (seeded); login → admin creates a title → uploads a video → publishes → it appears on home; non-admin visiting /admin is redirected; series episode navigation works.

## Acceptance criteria for Phase 3
- Full browsing and watching works as a guest, and the admin can manage everything from the UI without touching the API directly.
- Playwright suite passes.
- Phase summary provided in the Phase 0 format.
`
Phase 4: Hardening, Operations, and Launch Readiness
`

text
Build Phase 4. Phases 1–3 are complete. This phase makes the system safe and maintainable to run on a single server. No new user-facing features unless listed here.

## 1. Security hardening
- Rate limits: auth endpoints (login 5/min per IP+account, register 5/hour per IP, forgot-password 3/hour per email), stream endpoint, and admin upload endpoints. Use @nestjs/throttler with a SQLite-compatible in-memory store, documented.
- Helmet with HSTS in production. Verify CORS allow-list is env-driven.
- Audit log: new `AuditLog` table (actor, action, entity, entityId, metadata JSON, ip, createdAt). Log admin actions: role changes, deactivations, title publish/archive/delete, media delete, category changes, login failures (count only).
- Dependency audit: `pnpm audit` clean for high and critical. Document any exceptions.
- Input fuzzing check: run a short script that sends malformed JSON, oversized bodies (set express body limit to 1mb for JSON), wrong content types, and SQL-looking strings to every endpoint; no 500s.
- Secrets: fail fast on boot if required env vars are missing or weak (JWT secrets must be ≥32 chars and not equal to the example values).

## 2. Operations
- Health endpoints: GET `/api/health` (liveness) and `/api/health/ready` (checks DB connection and storage directory is writable). Use @nestjs/terminus.
- Structured logging with pino (nestjs-pino), request IDs (X-Request-Id), and log levels from env. No passwords or tokens in logs (redaction config).
- Graceful shutdown: close the DB, finish in-flight uploads or mark them FAILED on restart.
- Background job recovery: on boot, any MediaFile stuck in PROCESSING or UPLOADING for more than 30 minutes is marked FAILED with a message.
- Backup script: `scripts/backup.sh` that uses SQLite's online backup (`sqlite3 .backup`) and rsyncs `/storage` to a configured target, with retention of 7 daily backups.
- Database: enable WAL mode and foreign_keys=ON on connection; document pragmas in code, not manual steps.

## 3. Deployment
- Dockerfile for api and web (multi-stage, non-root user, ffmpeg installed in the api image).
- docker-compose.yml: api, web, a reverse proxy (Caddy or Nginx) with TLS, volumes for `/data` and `/storage`. The reverse proxy routes `/api/*` to api and everything else to web.
- Important: video streaming must go through the reverse proxy with buffering disabled for `/api/stream/`, and client_max_body_size or equivalent set to match MAX_VIDEO_SIZE_MB on the upload path.
- Production environment checklist in `docs/deploy.md`.

## 4. Quality gates
- CI config (GitHub Actions): install, lint, typecheck, unit tests, e2e tests, build for api and web, Docker build.
- Test coverage: at least 80% on the auth and uploads modules.
- Documentation: `README.md` (setup, scripts, env vars table), `docs/architecture.md` (diagram in Mermaid), `docs/permissions.md`, `docs/api.md` (link to Swagger plus a list of endpoints), `docs/deploy.md`, `docs/copyright.md`.

## 5. Legal and content safety (required)
- Add a Terms of Use page and a DMCA / copyright takedown page with a contact form that stores requests in a `TakedownRequest` table visible in the admin panel. Takedown action: admin can set a title to ARCHIVED and record the reason.
- Add a note in the README that uploaded content must be owned or licensed by the operator. This project does not include any licensed content.

## 6. Final review checklist (the agent must go through each item and report pass/fail)
- [ ] Every endpoint has auth decision documented (public, user, admin)
- [ ] No raw refresh tokens or passwords stored or logged
- [ ] Storage paths never exposed to clients
- [ ] Stream endpoint enforces PUBLISHED status
- [ ] Upload validation uses magic bytes
- [ ] Rate limits applied to all auth endpoints
- [ ] Admin actions are audit-logged
- [ ] Health checks work
- [ ] Docker compose up brings everything up from a clean machine
- [ ] All tests pass in CI

## Acceptance criteria for Phase 4
- `docker compose up --build` runs the full stack behind the proxy on localhost.
- The final review checklist is answered with evidence (command output or test names).
- Final phase summary includes a list of all known limitations and a recommended roadmap (watch history, favorites, payments, S3 storage, real email, transcoding to HLS, subtitles in Persian, etc.).
`
Tips for using Muse Spark 1.3 with these prompts:

Run one phase per session and don’t skip ahead. Phase 1 is the foundation; errors there will spread.
Start Phase 2 by asking the agent to confirm Phase 1 passes pnpm test.
For the upload and streaming parts, install ffmpeg on the machine first; the agent needs it for ffprobe and the seed video.
Only upload content you own or have a license for. The prompt includes copyright pages, but the legal responsibility is yours.